module

public import Linglib.Syntax.Tree.Command
public import Linglib.Syntax.Tree.Projection
public import Mathlib.Order.Atoms
public import Mathlib.Order.Hom.Set

/-!
# Barker & Pullum (1990): A Theory of Command Relations

Barker and Pullum generate a command relation from a property of nodes: `a` commands `b` when
every node with the property that properly dominates `a` dominates `b`. The literature's relations
differ only in the generator: S-command comes from the S nodes, K-command from S and NP,
MAX-command from the maximal projections, c-command from the branching nodes, and IDC-command from
every node. Mate relations intersect a command relation with its inverse, and government relations
intersect one with the composition of head-of and command.

The definition is `Syntax/Command.lean`'s, an upper polar in the generator, and the paper's
theorems are proved here from it. The Intersection Theorem and what follows from it are mathlib's
facts about polars; Descent, Fairness, Constituency and Embeddability are facts about the upper
bounds of a node's ancestors; and the maximal generator of a relation, which the paper defines as
a union over all relations generating the same command relation, is the closure of its upper
bounds under `lowerBounds ∘ upperBounds`.

Two questions the paper raises are settled. The characteristics it lists as common to all command
relations do not single them out, since dominance has all of them: a relation on a tree is
generated by a property exactly when it contains IDC-command and is fair. And its last footnote
leaves open whether the command relations carry a Boolean algebra. Ordered by inclusion, those
that properties generate do, corresponding with the order reversed to the sets of nodes that are
neither the root nor a leaf; those that relations generate, with the intersection and union the
paper gives them as meet and join, do not, since a relation can lack a complement.

Two of the paper's claims fail on its own definitions: the characterization of mates by equal
sets of upper bounds is strictly stronger than mutual command once a node may be its mate's upper
bound, and so is the prose characterization by equal command domains. On the paper's government
tree, whose maximal projections are read off its heads, Davis's definition of government fails
where the paper's restatement holds, and the government schema with head-of reaches the head of a
governed phrase.

## Main statements

* `isIntent_admits_iff`, `fair_le_and_not_isIntent_le`: a relation on a tree is generated by a
  property exactly when it contains the relation every node generates and is fair, and dominance,
  which is fair, is not so generated.
* `exists_forall_mem_commandRelation_iff_le`, `mem_commandRelation_Ici_iff`: on a tree what a node
  commands is the cone of one node, and command in a subtree is command in the tree.
* `maximalGenerator_iff`, `commandByRelation_union`: the maximal generator of a relation is a
  closure of its upper bounds, and on a tree the union of two relation-generated command relations
  is generated by the intersection of their maximal generators.
* `commandRelationIso`, `not_exists_isCompl_commandByRelation`: on a tree the command relations
  that properties generate are, under inclusion, the sets of internal nodes under reverse
  inclusion, while those that relations generate have no complements under intersection and union.

## Implementation notes

* A tree is a left-linear order with a least element that is well founded and in which every node
  but the root covers a mother; finite trees and the positions `TreePath` are instances.
* A relation is one that some property generates exactly when it is an intent of `Admits`,
  mathlib's `Order.IsIntent`, and closure under intersection is `Order.IsIntent.iInter`.
* A relation as generator pairs a bound with the commander it bounds, as in the paper, and
  counts only when the bound properly dominates the commander.
* The government tree leaves one maximal projection unexpanded in the paper; here it has a head.

## References

* [barker-pullum-1990]
* [klima-1964]
* [lasnik-1976]
* [rouveret-vergnaud-1980]
* [davis-1987]
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace BarkerPullum1990

open Core.Order PhraseStructure PhraseStructure.Tree
open RoseTree (IsBranchingAt cCommand)
open scoped SetRel

/-- In the government schema `a` governs `b` when `a` commands `b` and `b` bears to `a` the
composition of head-of with command. -/
def government {Node : Type*} (C : SetRel Node Node) (H : Node → Node → Prop) : SetRel Node Node :=
  {ab | ab.1 ~[C] ab.2 ∧ ∃ x, Relation.ReflTransGen H ab.2 x ∧ x ~[C] ab.1}

theorem government_subset {Node : Type*} (C : SetRel Node Node) (H : Node → Node → Prop) :
    government C H ⊆ C :=
  fun _ h ↦ h.1

/-! ### Trees

Definition 1 adopts Wall's definition of a tree. Dominance is reflexive and antisymmetric with a
single root; precedence is irreflexive, asymmetric and transitive; two nodes are related by
dominance exactly when neither precedes the other (the Exclusivity Condition); and precedence
passes to the nodes each relatum dominates (the Nontangling Condition). -/

/-- Positions under prefix dominance and branch-order precedence satisfy the conditions of
Definition 1 on dominance and precedence. -/
theorem treePath_isTree :
    (∀ a : TreePath, ⊥ ≤ a) ∧ IsStrictOrder TreePath TreePath.Precedes ∧
      (∀ a b : TreePath, (a ≤ b ∨ b ≤ a) ↔ ¬ (a.Precedes b ∨ b.Precedes a)) ∧
      ∀ ⦃a b c d : TreePath⦄, a.Precedes b → a ≤ c → b ≤ d → c.Precedes d := by
  refine ⟨fun _ ↦ bot_le, inferInstance, fun a b ↦ ⟨?_, fun h ↦ ?_⟩, fun _ _ _ _ h ↦ h.mono⟩
  · rintro (h | h) (hp | hp)
    exacts [hp.not_le h, hp.not_ge h, hp.not_ge h, hp.not_le h]
  · rcases TreePath.Precedes.trichotomy a b with h' | h' | h' | h'
    exacts [.inl h', .inr h', absurd (.inl h') h, absurd (.inr h') h]

-- The ancestors of a position are linearly ordered: the Connected Ancestor Condition of
-- Definition 15, under which command relations obey Constituency.
example : IsLeftLinear TreePath := inferInstance

variable {Node : Type*} [PartialOrder Node] {P Q : Set Node} {a b c m : Node}

/-! ### Command relations

By Definition 2 the upper bounds of `a` with respect to `P` are the members of `P` properly
dominating `a`, the set `P ∩ Set.Iio a`, and by Definition 3 `a` commands what every one of them
dominates, their upper bounds in the dominance order (`mem_commandRelation_iff_mem_upperBounds`).
As a function of `P` the relation is an upper polar, which gives Theorems 1 and 2. -/

/-- Theorem 1, the Intersection Theorem: a union of generators generates the intersection of
their relations. -/
theorem commandRelation_union (P Q : Set Node) :
    commandRelation (P ∪ Q) = commandRelation P ∩ commandRelation Q :=
  upperPolar_union ..

/-- Theorem 2: IDC-command, generated by every node, is the smallest command relation. -/
theorem commandRelation_univ_subset (P : Set Node) :
    commandRelation Set.univ ⊆ commandRelation P :=
  upperPolar_anti _ (Set.subset_univ P)

/-- On a tree `a` IDC-commands `b` exactly when the mother of `a` dominates `b`, the informal
definition (21). -/
theorem mem_commandRelation_univ_iff [IsLeftLinear Node] (ha : m ⋖ a) :
    a ~[commandRelation Set.univ] b ↔ m ≤ b :=
  mem_commandRelation_iff_le_of_covBy ha (Set.mem_univ m)

/-- Theorem 4, Boundedness: a root adds nothing to a generator. -/
theorem commandRelation_insert_of_isBot {r : Node} (hr : IsBot r) (P : Set Node) :
    commandRelation (insert r P) = commandRelation P :=
  (upperPolar_union _ {r} P).trans <| Set.inter_eq_right.2 fun _ _ _ hx _ ↦ hx ▸ hr _

/-- A relation that fails Descent, Theorem 5 and `mem_commandRelation_of_le`, is generated by no
property. -/
theorem not_isIntent_of_not_descent {R : SetRel Node Node} (hab : a ~[R] b) (hbc : b ≤ c)
    (hac : ¬ a ~[R] c) : ¬ Order.IsIntent Admits R := by
  rintro ⟨P, rfl⟩
  exact hac (mem_commandRelation_of_le hab hbc)

/-- A relation is fair when an upper bound is an upper bound for every node it properly
dominates. -/
def Fair (R : SetRel Node Node) : Prop :=
  ∀ a b c, a ~[R] b → b ~[R] c → ¬ a ~[R] c → ∀ d, a ~[R] d → b ≤ d

/-- Every command relation is fair (Theorem 6). -/
theorem fair_commandRelation (P : Set Node) : Fair (commandRelation P) := by
  intro a b c hab hbc hac d had
  simp only [mem_commandRelation, not_forall] at hac
  obtain ⟨x, hx, hxa, hxc⟩ := hac
  rcases (hab hx hxa).lt_or_eq with hxb | rfl
  · exact absurd (hbc hx hxb) hxc
  · exact had hx hxa

/-- On a rooted tree the nodes a node commands are exactly those some node dominates (Theorem 7). -/
theorem exists_forall_mem_commandRelation_iff_le [OrderBot Node] [IsLeftLinear Node]
    [WellFoundedLT Node] [IsStronglyCoatomic Node] (P : Set Node) (a : Node) :
    ∃ x, ∀ b, a ~[commandRelation P] b ↔ x ≤ b := by
  induction a using WellFoundedLT.induction with | ind a ih
  rcases eq_or_ne a ⊥ with rfl | ha
  · exact ⟨⊥, fun b ↦ ⟨fun _ ↦ bot_le, fun _ _ _ hx ↦ absurd hx not_lt_bot⟩⟩
  · obtain ⟨m, -, hm⟩ := exists_le_covBy_of_lt (bot_lt_iff_ne_bot.2 ha)
    by_cases hP : m ∈ P
    · exact ⟨m, fun b ↦ mem_commandRelation_iff_le_of_covBy hm hP⟩
    · obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := ih m hm.lt
      exact ⟨x, fun b ↦ (mem_commandRelation_iff_of_covBy hm hP).trans (hx b)⟩

/-- Command among the nodes a node dominates is command in the whole tree (Theorem 8). -/
theorem mem_commandRelation_Ici_iff [IsLeftLinear Node] (r : Node) (P : Set Node)
    (a b : Set.Ici r) :
    a ~[commandRelation (Subtype.val ⁻¹' P)] b ↔ a.val ~[commandRelation P] b.val :=
  ⟨fun h x hx hxa ↦ (IsLeftLinear.comparable_of_le_common hxa.le a.2).elim
      (fun hxr ↦ hxr.trans b.2) fun hrx ↦ h (a := ⟨x, hrx⟩) hx hxa,
    fun h _ hx hxa ↦ h hx hxa⟩

/-! ### Characterizing command relations

The paper lists reflexivity, Descent, Constituency and Fairness among the characteristics common
to all command relations. They do not single the command relations out, since dominance has all
of them. What does is Fairness together with Theorem 2: a relation on a tree is generated by a
property exactly when it contains IDC-command and is fair, and Descent and Constituency follow. -/

theorem fair_iff {R : SetRel Node Node} :
    Fair R ↔ ∀ a b, a ~[R] b → (∀ c, b ~[R] c → a ~[R] c) ∨ ∀ d, a ~[R] d → b ≤ d := by
  refine ⟨fun h a b hab ↦ or_iff_not_imp_left.2 fun hn d had ↦ ?_,
    fun h a b c hab hbc hac ↦ (h a b hab).resolve_left fun h' ↦ hac (h' c hbc)⟩
  obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ := not_forall.1 hn
  exact h a b c hab (not_imp.1 hc).1 (not_imp.1 hc).2 d had

/-- A relation on a tree is generated by a property exactly when it contains IDC-command and is
fair. Fairness makes a daughter command no more than her mother, hence either what her mother
dominates or all her mother commands, and makes sisters agree on which; so the relation obeys the
rules that fix the command relation of the mothers that bound a daughter. -/
theorem isIntent_admits_iff [OrderBot Node] [IsLeftLinear Node] [WellFoundedLT Node]
    [IsStronglyCoatomic Node] {R : SetRel Node Node} :
    Order.IsIntent Admits R ↔ commandRelation Set.univ ⊆ R ∧ Fair R := by
  refine ⟨?_, fun ⟨hI, hF⟩ ↦ ?_⟩
  · rintro ⟨P, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨commandRelation_univ_subset P, fair_commandRelation P⟩
  rw [fair_iff] at hF
  have hle : ∀ ⦃a b⦄, a ≤ b → a ~[R] b := fun _ _ h ↦ hI fun _ _ hxa ↦ (hxa.trans_le h).le
  have hcov : ∀ ⦃m a b⦄, m ⋖ a → m ≤ b → a ~[R] b := fun _ _ _ hm hb ↦
    hI (mem_commandRelation_of_covBy hm hb)
  have hsub : ∀ ⦃m a⦄, m ⋖ a → ∀ b, a ~[R] b → m ~[R] b := fun m a hm ↦
    (hF m a (hle hm.le)).resolve_right fun h ↦ hm.lt.not_ge (h m (hle le_rfl))
  have hedge : ∀ ⦃m a⦄, m ⋖ a → (∀ b, a ~[R] b ↔ m ≤ b) ∨ ∀ b, a ~[R] b ↔ m ~[R] b :=
    fun m a hm ↦ (hF a m (hcov hm le_rfl)).symm.imp (fun h b ↦ ⟨h b, hcov hm⟩)
      fun h b ↦ ⟨hsub hm b, h b⟩
  refine ⟨{m | ∃ a, m ⋖ a ∧ ∀ b, a ~[R] b ↔ m ≤ b},
    (eq_commandRelation_of_forall_covBy (fun b ↦ hle bot_le) ?_ ?_).symm⟩
  · rintro m a hm ⟨a₀, -, h₀⟩ b
    refine (hedge hm).elim (fun h ↦ h b) fun h ↦ (h b).trans ⟨fun hmb ↦ ?_, fun hb ↦ hle hb⟩
    exact (hF a₀ a ((h₀ a).2 hm.le)).elim (fun h' ↦ (h₀ b).1 (h' b ((h b).2 hmb)))
      fun h' ↦ absurd (h' m ((h₀ m).2 le_rfl)) hm.lt.not_ge
  · exact fun m a hm hP b ↦ (hedge hm).elim (fun h ↦ absurd ⟨a, hm, h⟩ hP) fun h ↦ h b

theorem descent_and_constituency_of_fair [OrderBot Node] [IsLeftLinear Node] [WellFoundedLT Node]
    [IsStronglyCoatomic Node] {R : SetRel Node Node} (hI : commandRelation Set.univ ⊆ R)
    (hF : Fair R) :
    (∀ a b c, a ~[R] b → b ≤ c → a ~[R] c) ∧ ∀ a, ∃ x, ∀ b, a ~[R] b ↔ x ≤ b := by
  obtain ⟨P, rfl⟩ := isIntent_admits_iff.2 ⟨hI, hF⟩
  exact ⟨fun _ _ _ ↦ mem_commandRelation_of_le, exists_forall_mem_commandRelation_iff_le P⟩

/-- Dominance is fair, and like every order it is reflexive and satisfies Descent and
Constituency; but on a tree with an edge no property generates it, since a node commands its
mother and does not dominate her. -/
theorem fair_le_and_not_isIntent_le [IsLeftLinear Node] (h : m ⋖ a) :
    Fair {ab : Node × Node | ab.1 ≤ ab.2} ∧
      ¬ Order.IsIntent Admits {ab : Node × Node | ab.1 ≤ ab.2} := by
  refine ⟨fun _ _ _ hab hbc hac ↦ absurd (le_trans hab hbc) hac, ?_⟩
  rintro ⟨P, hP⟩
  exact h.lt.not_ge (hP ▸ mem_commandRelation_of_covBy (P := P) h le_rfl :
    (a, m) ∈ {ab : Node × Node | ab.1 ≤ ab.2})

-- The characterization holds of the positions of phrase structure trees.
example {R : SetRel TreePath TreePath} :
    Order.IsIntent Admits R ↔ commandRelation Set.univ ⊆ R ∧ Fair R :=
  isIntent_admits_iff

/-! ### The literature's relations -/

section Labeled

variable {C W : Type*} [DecidableEq C]

/-- Rouveret and Vergnaud's c-command is generated by the branching nodes together with the
maximal projections. -/
abbrev rvCommand (t : Tree C W) : SetRel TreePath TreePath :=
  commandRelation ({p | IsBranchingAt t p} ∪ Tree.maximalProjectionsAt t)

theorem rvCommand_eq (t : Tree C W) : rvCommand t = cCommand t ∩ Tree.maxCommand t :=
  upperPolar_union ..

/-- NP-command is generated by the NP nodes, the maximal projections of nouns. -/
abbrev npCommand (t : Tree Cat W) : SetRel TreePath TreePath :=
  commandRelation (labeled t {.N} ∩ Tree.maximalProjectionsAt t)

/-- Lasnik's kommand is generated by the cyclic nodes, S and NP. -/
abbrev kCommand (t : Tree Cat W) : SetRel TreePath TreePath :=
  commandRelation (labeled t {.S} ∪ (labeled t {.N} ∩ Tree.maximalProjectionsAt t))

theorem kCommand_eq (t : Tree Cat W) : kCommand t = sCommand t ∩ npCommand t :=
  upperPolar_union ..

end Labeled

/-- Klima's in-construction-with is the inverse of c-command. -/
abbrev inConstructionWith {W : Type*} (t : Tree Cat W) : SetRel TreePath TreePath :=
  (cCommand t).inv

instance {W : Type*} (t : Tree Cat W) (ab : TreePath × TreePath) :
    Decidable (ab ∈ inConstructionWith t) :=
  inferInstanceAs (Decidable (ab.2 ~[cCommand t] ab.1))

/-- In `[S [NP N N] V]` the verb is in construction with the NP, which dominates its first
noun, but not with that noun. -/
def witness : Tree Cat Unit :=
  .node .S [.node .N [.terminal .N (), .terminal .N ()], .terminal .V ()]

theorem not_isIntent_inConstructionWith :
    ¬ Order.IsIntent Admits (inConstructionWith witness) :=
  not_isIntent_of_not_descent (a := ⟨[1]⟩) (b := ⟨[0]⟩) (c := ⟨[0, 0]⟩) (by decide) (by decide)
    (by decide)

/-! ### C-command through the mother relation

Definition 9 takes the mother relation to be covering in the dominance order, and Definition 10
takes c-command to be generated by the nodes that are mothers of two distinct nodes. On the
positions of a tree these are the positions with at least two children, so the relation is the
substrate's `RoseTree.cCommand`. -/

section Mother

open RoseTree

/-- The branching nodes of Definition 10: the mothers of two distinct nodes. -/
def branchingNodes : Set Node := {n | ∃ a b, a ≠ b ∧ n ⋖ a ∧ n ⋖ b}

/-- Definition 10: c-command is generated by the branching nodes. -/
def cCommand : SetRel Node Node := commandRelation (branchingNodes (Node := Node))

theorem mem_commandRelation_subtype_iff {D : Set Node} (hD : IsLowerSet D) (P : Set Node)
    (a b : D) :
    a ~[commandRelation (Subtype.val ⁻¹' P)] b ↔ a.val ~[commandRelation P] b.val :=
  ⟨fun h x hx hxa ↦ h (a := ⟨x, hD hxa.le a.2⟩) hx hxa, fun h _ hx hxa ↦ h hx hxa⟩

variable {β : Type*}

theorem mem_branchingNodes_iff {t : RoseTree β} (p : t.Positions) :
    p ∈ branchingNodes ↔ IsBranchingAt t p.val := by
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ : ∃ s, t.subtreeAt p.val.toList = some s := Option.isSome_iff_exists.mp p.2
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨⟨⟨la⟩, ha'⟩, ⟨⟨lb⟩, hb'⟩, hab, ha, hb⟩
    obtain ⟨i, hi⟩ := TreePath.covBy_iff.mp (Positions.covBy_iff.mp ha)
    obtain ⟨j, hj⟩ := TreePath.covBy_iff.mp (Positions.covBy_iff.mp hb)
    simp only at hi hj
    subst hi hj
    obtain ⟨s₁, hs₁, hi'⟩ := mem_validPaths_append_singleton_iff.mp ha'
    obtain ⟨s₂, hs₂, hj'⟩ := mem_validPaths_append_singleton_iff.mp hb'
    rw [hs, Option.some.injEq] at hs₁ hs₂
    subst hs₁ hs₂
    have hij : i ≠ j := fun h ↦ hab (by subst h; rfl)
    exact ⟨s, hs, show 2 ≤ s.children.length by omega⟩
  · rintro ⟨s', hs', hlen⟩
    rw [Option.mem_def, hs, Option.some.injEq] at hs'
    subst hs'
    rw [arity] at hlen
    refine ⟨⟨⟨p.val.toList ++ [0]⟩, mem_validPaths_append_singleton_iff.mpr ⟨s, hs, by omega⟩⟩,
      ⟨⟨p.val.toList ++ [1]⟩, mem_validPaths_append_singleton_iff.mpr ⟨s, hs, by omega⟩⟩,
      fun h ↦ ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
    · simpa using congrArg (fun q : t.Positions ↦ q.val.toList) h
    · exact Positions.covBy_iff.mpr (TreePath.covBy_iff.mpr ⟨0, rfl⟩)
    · exact Positions.covBy_iff.mpr (TreePath.covBy_iff.mpr ⟨1, rfl⟩)

theorem branchingNodes_eq_preimage (t : RoseTree β) :
    (branchingNodes : Set t.Positions) = Subtype.val ⁻¹' {p | IsBranchingAt t p} :=
  Set.ext fun p ↦ mem_branchingNodes_iff p

theorem mem_cCommand_iff {t : RoseTree β} (a b : t.Positions) :
    a ~[cCommand] b ↔ a.val ~[t.cCommand] b.val := by
  rw [cCommand, branchingNodes_eq_preimage]
  exact mem_commandRelation_subtype_iff (isLowerSet_validPaths t) _ a b

end Mother

/-! ### Mates -/

/-- Clausemates are S-mates. -/
abbrev clausemates {W : Type*} (t : Tree Cat W) : SetRel TreePath TreePath :=
  mateRelation (labeled t {.S})

theorem mateRelation_empty : mateRelation (∅ : Set Node) = commandRelation ∅ := by
  ext ⟨a, b⟩
  simp [mem_mateRelation]

/-- In `[S [S N N] V]` a noun and the S above it are clausemates. -/
def nested : Tree Cat Unit :=
  .node .S [.node .S [.terminal .N (), .terminal .N ()], .terminal .V ()]

theorem clausemates_not_sameUpperBounds :
    (⟨[0, 0]⟩, ⟨[0]⟩) ∈ clausemates nested ∧
      labeled nested {.S} ∩ Set.Iio ⟨[0, 0]⟩ ≠ labeled nested {.S} ∩ Set.Iio ⟨[0]⟩ :=
  ⟨by decide, fun h ↦ (by decide : (⟨[0]⟩ : TreePath) ∉
    labeled nested {.S} ∩ Set.Iio ⟨[0]⟩) (h ▸ by decide)⟩

theorem clausemates_not_sameDomain :
    (⟨[0]⟩, ⟨[1]⟩) ∈ sCommand nested ∧ (⟨[0, 0]⟩, ⟨[1]⟩) ∉ sCommand nested := by
  constructor <;> decide

/-! ### Government

A governor is a minimal projection related to what it governs through the maximal projections,
here read off the tree's heads, one of the "more tree-dependent" ways of defining MAX that Barker
and Pullum allow. The head-of relation of the government schema is the head-daughter relation
reversed. -/

/-- On Davis's definition (24b) `a` governs `b` when every maximal projection dominates `b` iff it
dominates `a`. -/
def davis (max : Set Node) (a b : Node) : Prop := ∀ P ∈ max, P ≤ b ↔ P ≤ a

/-- The restatement (26c–d) of Davis's definition asks that `a` and `b` be MAX-mates and that
neither dominate the other. -/
def davisRestated (max : Set Node) (a b : Node) : Prop :=
  a ~[mateRelation max] b ∧ ¬ a ≤ b ∧ ¬ b ≤ a

/-- By the restatement (26), `a` governs `b` in `t` when `a` is a minimal projection, of bar
level 0, and the two are MAX-mates neither dominating the other. Clause (b), on INFL, is left out,
since no category here is INFL. -/
def Governs {W : Type*} (t : Tree Cat W) (a b : TreePath) : Prop :=
  IsMinimalProjection (Tree.HeadDaughterAt t) a ∧ davisRestated (Tree.maximalProjectionsAt t) a b

/-- Tree (25) has `X²` over `X¹` and `Y²`, and `X¹` over `X⁰` alone; here `X` is a verb and `Y` a
noun, and `Y²`, which the figure leaves unexpanded, has a head `Y⁰`. -/
def tree25 : Tree Cat Unit :=
  .node .V [.node .V [.terminal .V ()], .node .N [.terminal .N ()]]

theorem tree25_davis_fails : ¬ davis (Tree.maximalProjectionsAt tree25) ⟨[0, 0]⟩ ⟨[1]⟩ :=
  fun h ↦ absurd ((h ⟨[1]⟩ (by decide)).1 le_rfl) (by decide)

theorem tree25_governs : Governs tree25 ⟨[0, 0]⟩ ⟨[1]⟩ :=
  ⟨by decide, by decide, by decide, by decide⟩

theorem tree25_not_governs_bar :
    davisRestated (Tree.maximalProjectionsAt tree25) ⟨[0]⟩ ⟨[1]⟩ ∧ ¬ Governs tree25 ⟨[0]⟩ ⟨[1]⟩ :=
  ⟨⟨by decide, by decide, by decide⟩, fun h ↦ absurd h.1 (by decide)⟩

theorem tree25_governs_head :
    (⟨[0, 0]⟩, ⟨[1, 0]⟩) ∈
      government (Tree.maxCommand tree25) (flip (Tree.HeadDaughterAt tree25)) :=
  ⟨by decide, ⟨[1]⟩, .single ⟨0, by decide, rfl⟩, by decide⟩

/-! ### Relations as generators

Definition 19 lets the candidates for bounding a commander depend on it: the upper bounds of `a`
with respect to a relation `R` are the nodes that properly dominate `a` and bear `R` to it. -/

/-- The command relation a relation `R` generates: `a` commands `b` when every node that properly
dominates `a` and bears `R` to it dominates `b`. -/
def commandByRelation (R : Node → Node → Prop) : SetRel Node Node :=
  {ab | ab.2 ∈ upperBounds {x | x < ab.1 ∧ R x ab.1}}

theorem mem_commandByRelation {R : Node → Node → Prop} :
    a ~[commandByRelation R] b ↔ ∀ x, x < a → R x a → x ≤ b :=
  ⟨fun h _ hxa hR ↦ h ⟨hxa, hR⟩, fun h x hx ↦ h x hx.1 hx.2⟩

theorem commandByRelation_mem (P : Set Node) :
    commandByRelation (fun x _ ↦ x ∈ P) = commandRelation P :=
  Set.ext fun _ ↦ ⟨fun h _ hx hxa ↦ h ⟨hxa, hx⟩, fun h _ hx ↦ h hx.2 hx.1⟩

/-- Relations generate fair command relations no more than by accident. This generator gives the
first noun of `witness` its NP as sole bound and every other node none. -/
def unfairGenerator (x a : TreePath) : Prop := x = ⟨[0]⟩ ∧ a = ⟨[0, 0]⟩

theorem not_fair_unfairGenerator : ¬ Fair (commandByRelation unfairGenerator) := fun h ↦
  absurd (h ⟨[0, 0]⟩ ⟨[0, 1]⟩ ⟨[1]⟩ (fun _ hx ↦ hx.2.1 ▸ by decide)
    (fun _ hx ↦ absurd hx.2.2 (by decide)) (fun h ↦ absurd (h ⟨by decide, rfl, rfl⟩) (by decide))
    ⟨[0, 0]⟩ (fun _ hx ↦ hx.2.1 ▸ by decide)) (by decide)

/-- Definition 21: the maximal generator of `R` is the union of the relations containing `R` that
generate the same command relation. -/
def maximalGenerator (R : Node → Node → Prop) : Node → Node → Prop :=
  fun x a ↦ ∃ S, R ≤ S ∧ commandByRelation S = commandByRelation R ∧ S x a

theorem le_maximalGenerator (R : Node → Node → Prop) : R ≤ maximalGenerator R :=
  fun _ _ h ↦ ⟨R, le_rfl, rfl, h⟩

/-- The maximal generator relates to `a` whatever does not properly dominate it, and of its proper
dominators those in the closure of its upper bounds under `lowerBounds ∘ upperBounds`: the nodes
dominating everything `a` commands. -/
theorem maximalGenerator_iff {R : Node → Node → Prop} {x : Node} :
    maximalGenerator R x a ↔
      (x < a → x ∈ lowerBounds (upperBounds {y | y < a ∧ R y a})) := by
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨S, -, hS, hSxa⟩ hxa b hb
    exact (hS ▸ (show a ~[commandByRelation R] b from hb) : a ~[commandByRelation S] b)
      ⟨hxa, hSxa⟩
  · refine fun h ↦ ⟨fun x a ↦ x < a → x ∈ lowerBounds (upperBounds {y | y < a ∧ R y a}),
      fun _ _ hR hxa ↦ subset_lowerBounds_upperBounds _ ⟨hxa, hR⟩, Set.ext fun _ ↦ ⟨fun hb _ hx ↦
        hb ⟨hx.1, fun _ ↦ subset_lowerBounds_upperBounds _ hx⟩, fun hb _ hx ↦ hx.2 hx.1 hb⟩, h⟩

theorem commandByRelation_maximalGenerator (R : Node → Node → Prop) :
    commandByRelation (maximalGenerator R) = commandByRelation R :=
  Set.ext fun _ ↦ ⟨fun h _ hx ↦ h ⟨hx.1, le_maximalGenerator R _ _ hx.2⟩,
    fun h _ hx ↦ maximalGenerator_iff.1 hx.2 hx.1 h⟩

/-- On a tree the union of two command relations is generated by the intersection of their maximal
generators (Theorem 9). -/
theorem commandByRelation_union [IsLeftLinear Node] (R S : Node → Node → Prop) :
    commandByRelation R ∪ commandByRelation S =
      commandByRelation fun x a ↦ maximalGenerator R x a ∧ maximalGenerator S x a := by
  ext ⟨a, b⟩
  constructor
  · rintro (h | h) x ⟨hxa, hR, hS⟩
    exacts [maximalGenerator_iff.1 hR hxa h, maximalGenerator_iff.1 hS hxa h]
  · intro h
    by_contra hn
    simp only [Set.mem_union, mem_commandByRelation, not_or, not_forall, exists_prop] at hn
    obtain ⟨⟨c, hca, hRc, hcb⟩, d, hda, hSd, hdb⟩ := hn
    rcases IsLeftLinear.comparable_of_le_common hca.le hda.le with hcd | hdc
    · exact hcb (h ⟨hca, le_maximalGenerator R c a hRc,
        maximalGenerator_iff.2 fun _ _ hu ↦ hcd.trans (hu ⟨hda, hSd⟩)⟩)
    · exact hdb (h ⟨hda, maximalGenerator_iff.2 fun _ _ hu ↦ hdc.trans (hu ⟨hca, hRc⟩),
        le_maximalGenerator S d a hSd⟩)

/-- The command relations generated by relations form a lattice by Theorem 9, but not a Boolean one.
-/
theorem not_exists_isCompl_commandByRelation :
    ¬ ∃ S : Fin 4 → Fin 4 → Prop,
      commandByRelation (fun x a ↦ x = 1 ∧ a = 3) ∩ commandByRelation S =
          commandByRelation (fun _ _ ↦ True) ∧
        commandByRelation (fun x a ↦ x = 1 ∧ a = 3) ∪ commandByRelation S = Set.univ := by
  rintro ⟨S, hinf, hsup⟩
  have h0 : (3 : Fin 4) ~[commandByRelation S] 0 :=
    ((Set.ext_iff.1 hsup (3, 0)).2 trivial).resolve_left fun h ↦
      absurd (mem_commandByRelation.1 h 1 (by decide) ⟨rfl, rfl⟩) (by decide)
  have h1 : (3 : Fin 4) ~[commandByRelation S] 1 := fun _ hx ↦ (h0 hx).trans (by decide)
  have h1' : (3 : Fin 4) ~[commandByRelation fun x a ↦ x = 1 ∧ a = 3] 1 := fun _ hx ↦ hx.2.1.le
  exact absurd (mem_commandByRelation.1 ((Set.ext_iff.1 hinf (3, 1)).1 ⟨h1', h1⟩) 2 (by decide)
    trivial) (by decide)

/-! ### The lattice of command relations

The relations that properties generate are closed under intersection and have a least member,
and the paper's last footnote asks whether command relations carry a Boolean algebra. On a tree a
generator matters only through its internal nodes, and distinct sets of internal nodes generate
distinct relations, so under inclusion these relations are the sets of internal nodes under
reverse inclusion. The join in that order is generated by the intersection of the
generators; as the paper notes, the union of two such relations need not be one. -/

section Lattice

variable [OrderBot Node]

variable (Node) in
/-- The internal nodes are those that are neither the root nor a leaf. -/
def internalNodes : Set Node := {x | x ≠ ⊥ ∧ ¬ IsMax x}

theorem commandRelation_inter_internalNodes (P : Set Node) :
    commandRelation (P ∩ internalNodes Node) = commandRelation P := by
  ext ⟨a, b⟩
  refine ⟨fun h x hx hxa ↦ ?_, fun h _ hx hxa ↦ h hx.1 hxa⟩
  rcases eq_or_ne x ⊥ with rfl | hx0
  · exact bot_le
  · exact h ⟨hx, hx0, fun hmax ↦ hmax.not_lt hxa⟩ hxa

variable [IsLeftLinear Node] [WellFoundedLT Node] [IsStronglyCoatomic Node]

theorem commandRelation_subset_commandRelation_iff :
    commandRelation P ⊆ commandRelation Q ↔ Q ∩ internalNodes Node ⊆ P := by
  refine ⟨fun h x ⟨hxQ, hx0, hxmax⟩ ↦ ?_, fun h ↦ ?_⟩
  · by_contra hxP
    obtain ⟨m, -, hm⟩ := exists_le_covBy_of_lt (bot_lt_iff_ne_bot.2 hx0)
    obtain ⟨y, hxy⟩ := not_isMax_iff.1 hxmax
    obtain ⟨a, hxa, -⟩ := exists_covBy_le_of_lt hxy
    have hP : a ~[commandRelation P] m :=
      (mem_commandRelation_iff_of_covBy hxa hxP).2 (mem_commandRelation_of_covBy hm le_rfl)
    exact hm.lt.not_ge (h hP hxQ hxa.lt)
  · rw [← commandRelation_inter_internalNodes Q]
    exact upperPolar_anti _ h

theorem commandRelation_eq_commandRelation_iff :
    commandRelation P = commandRelation Q ↔
      P ∩ internalNodes Node = Q ∩ internalNodes Node := by
  rw [Set.Subset.antisymm_iff, commandRelation_subset_commandRelation_iff,
    commandRelation_subset_commandRelation_iff, Set.Subset.antisymm_iff]
  exact ⟨fun h ↦ ⟨Set.subset_inter h.2 Set.inter_subset_right,
      Set.subset_inter h.1 Set.inter_subset_right⟩,
    fun h ↦ ⟨h.2.trans Set.inter_subset_left, h.1.trans Set.inter_subset_left⟩⟩

/-- On a tree the command relations that properties generate, ordered by inclusion, are the sets
of internal nodes ordered by reverse inclusion, and so a Boolean algebra. -/
noncomputable def commandRelationIso :
    (Set (internalNodes Node))ᵒᵈ ≃o Set.range (commandRelation (α := Node)) :=
  let f : (Set (internalNodes Node))ᵒᵈ ↪o SetRel Node Node :=
    OrderEmbedding.ofMapLEIff (fun S ↦ commandRelation (Subtype.val '' S.ofDual)) fun S T ↦ by
      rw [commandRelation_subset_commandRelation_iff,
        Set.inter_eq_left.2 (Subtype.coe_image_subset _ _),
        Set.image_subset_image_iff Subtype.val_injective]
      exact Iff.rfl
  f.orderIso.trans <| Set.orderIsoOfEq _ _ <| by
    ext R
    constructor
    · rintro ⟨S, rfl⟩
      exact ⟨_, rfl⟩
    · rintro ⟨P, rfl⟩
      refine ⟨.toDual (Subtype.val ⁻¹' P), ?_⟩
      change commandRelation (Subtype.val '' (Subtype.val ⁻¹' P)) = commandRelation P
      rw [Subtype.image_preimage_coe, Set.inter_comm, commandRelation_inter_internalNodes]

/-- On the chain of three nodes the middle one is internal, so adding it to a generator shrinks the
relation, while adding the leaf changes nothing. -/
example :
    ¬ commandRelation (∅ : Set (Fin 3)) ⊆ commandRelation {1} ∧
      commandRelation ({2} : Set (Fin 3)) = commandRelation ∅ := by
  rw [commandRelation_subset_commandRelation_iff, commandRelation_eq_commandRelation_iff]
  refine ⟨fun h ↦ h ⟨rfl, by decide, by decide⟩, ?_⟩
  ext x
  simp only [internalNodes, Set.mem_inter_iff, Set.mem_singleton_iff, Set.mem_ofPred_eq,
    Set.mem_empty_iff_false, false_and, iff_false, not_and, not_not]
  rintro rfl -
  decide

end Lattice

end BarkerPullum1990
